--- the nofail failure mode, demonstrated ---
    An entry pointing at a host that does not exist, WITHOUT nofail.
    On a real boot systemd would block on this; here mount -a shows the
    same failure directly, bounded so the drill does not hang.

    (192.0.2.0/24 is TEST-NET-1 from RFC 5737 - guaranteed unroutable)
    attempting the mount with a 20s ceiling:
      mount error(115): Operation now in progress
      Refer to the mount.cifs(8) manual page (e.g. man mount.cifs) and kernel log messages (dmesg)
      ...gave up after 10s
[PASS] the unreachable share did NOT mount, as expected

    Without nofail this hang happens during boot, on the critical path.
    With nofail the same failure is logged and boot proceeds. With
    x-systemd.automount it is not even attempted until first access.
    (removed the deliberately broken entry)

--- systemd automount option ---
    x-systemd.automount is present in the fstab entry, syntactically
    correct, and accepted by mount. On a systemd host the generator
    turns it into an automount unit that mounts the share on first
    access rather than at boot, which is what keeps a slow or absent
    server from delaying startup. This container runs smbd as PID 1,
    so the option is carried in the entry and read by mount here.

RESULT: PERSISTENCE CONFIGURED AND VERIFIED

